 mechanical calculator","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"b8dff7f5-1d1d-5fcd-a63b-8bff65bd4f2b","text":"A paper-only information system","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"d7b7baab-b143-5ea1-b70f-e33791e41a18","text":"A general-purpose system with no measurement input","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"03210986-4268-523e-afdd-905bb476df90","text":"A hybrid computing system","isCorrect":true,"position":3}]},{"id":"26df9c16-5604-51f7-8cfa-22ac4ce9a393","position":18,"prompt":"Why is classifying computers solely by physical size less useful today than it once was?","explanation":"Miniaturization and distributed computing have weakened the direct link between physical size and capability.","questionKind":"application","difficulty":"hard","cognitive":"analysis","topic":"History, Generations and Classification of Computers","fingerprint":"de7d67c14019704584061267869160f597a175405beb6b82bbf6e0d368cbdfa2","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"COS 101 lecture note, Module One","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFile":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_cos101_200_v1","sourceReference":"COS 101 lecture note, Module One","technicalAccuracyReviewCompleted":true,"humanVerificationRequired":true},"options":[{"id":"2e7eea89-7c9f-595e-bcc8-e44a36bcc510","text":"All computers now perform identical tasks","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"35c4a356-3395-53cf-b8e0-25a0d6c4a542","text":"Processing power is determined only by colour","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"690aa80e-3579-5e30-b930-56e1b4c79d6d","text":"Small devices can now provide capabilities once limited to much larger machines","isCorrect":true,"position":2},{"id":"7f386dee-c658-5a69-8344-767fa0ea59e6","text":"Computers no longer have physical dimensions","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"93b685d2-d921-5022-a9cd-cfcfa61c8e6f","position":19,"prompt":"Which trend best summarizes the progression described from early to later computer generations?","explanation":"Advances from tubes to transistors, ICs and microprocessors improved speed, size, cost and reliability.","questionKind":"application","difficulty":"hard","cognitive":"analysis","topic":"History, Generations and Classification of Computers","fingerprint":"365d6a5f7e85ef68a4895979e11ebcc60c8742f8eb78fed9880957fb89a282ea","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"COS 101 lecture note, Module One","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFile":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_cos101_200_v1","sourceReference":"COS 101 lecture note, Module One","technicalAccuracyReviewCompleted":true,"humanVerificationRequired":true},"options":[{"id":"af3bf42b-143c-513e-b5ab-851e7966b799","text":"The elimination of software and networking","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"b3dd10f3-f467-5435-9aa5-d25ae1c67cbd","text":"Smaller, faster, more reliable systems with greater integration","isCorrect":true,"position":1},{"id":"6d8654b0-9342-5098-b083-d0e30027613e","text":"Larger systems with less memory and higher power use","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"11eeadf1-2ffd-5f80-a807-1dadfafa9bdd","text":"A steady return from electronic to manual processing","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"3a7352bf-663f-5173-be12-3da47308deca","position":20,"prompt":"What is an algorithm?","explanation":"Algorithms describe precise steps that can be followed to transform input into a result.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"History, Generations and Classification of Computers","fingerprint":"23c773fb483d7856b2164e6feb453abcc40f7896de4dde123f572b190fb49ce7","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"General COS 101 enrichment: History, Generations and Classification of Computers","sourceKind":"general_cos101","reviewedFromLocalFile":false},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_cos101_200_v1","sourceReference":"General COS 101 enrichment: History, Generations and Classification of Computers","technicalAccuracyReviewCompleted":true,"humanVerificationRequired":true},"options":[{"id":"455610af-dc30-5c